Editorial & rhetorical recommendations (thesis quality and credibility)

Explicitly separate interpretive layers:
Label sections clearly as “Scriptural/Interpretive reading” and “Physical/Empirical evidence.” Readers should be able to tell when you are arguing exegesis vs scientific mechanism.
Tone & claims:
Where you make strong empirical claims (specific percentages, timelines, or single‑event catastrophic outcomes), either cite sources or rephrase into conditional language and provide uncertainty ranges.
Avoid repeating identical absolute claims across chapters — tighten to avoid redundancy.
Address counterarguments:
Add a short “Objections & responses” appendix (e.g., skepticism about MICI speed, potential for adaptation/mitigation, geoengineering arguments). Respond briefly with evidence or explain why your framing stands.
Use a references system:
Convert expansion_refs into a properly formatted references file (refs.bib or refs.md) and cite inline (numbered footnotes or parenthetical).
Add a “How to read the evidence” note explaining your selection criteria.
Technical / repository / site structure recommendations

File format and naming:
Convert the chapter files to Markdown (.md) for easier editing, templating, and ebook generation. Use consistent names: chapter-01-babylon.md, chapter-02-tipping-points.md, etc. Remove spaces and punctuation from filenames (rename "chapter10a - norain" → chapter-10a-norain.md).
Content templates:
Use a per‑chapter frontmatter template (title, short description, keywords, canonical URL, image) so the static site generator or GitHub Pages can produce consistent pages AND an automatic TOC.
Standardize the top of each chapter: one-line thesis, 3–4 bullet summary, estimated reading time, list of key citations.
Navigation & index:
Split the overly long index.html into a concise index + per‑chapter pages. Keep a short introduction and TOC on index; each chapter lives at its own URL with prev/next links.
Add a clickable TOC and in‑page anchor links for long chapters.
Accessibility & SEO:
Add alt text to images (you already have alt text but ensure all images have it). Ensure headings follow H1→H2→H3 order and add aria roles where helpful.
Update metadata to your chosen title "GLOBAL WARMING - The Destruction of Babylon" across index.html, README, and JSON‑LD.
Visuals & figures:
Add data figures (global temp graph, permafrost carbon map, port elevation map) with captions and source links. Prefer SVG or PNGs with appropriate resolution and a “source” link.
Ebook production:
Use a simple static site generator (Jekyll, Hugo, or a minimal HTML template) or pandoc pipeline to produce EPUB/PDF from the Markdown chapters. Add a makefile or script to build the ebook.
Code / repo hygiene:
Remove tracking scripts from the public repo if you want privacy‑respecting distribution (the index has Google Analytics). If you keep analytics, add a privacy note.
Add a references license/attribution page for reused images.
